+// --- Guest audio mixer boost (Switch) ----------------------------------------
+//
+// Why: the host "Audio Worker" (0x2B) runs the guest render callback twice per
+// 5.33 ms output period (two render clients, 256 samples at 48 kHz; with
+// audio_parallel_client_callbacks each client has its own worker, "Audio Worker"
+// and "Audio Worker 1", and the two callbacks overlap). That guest
+// callback (sub_822A9968) KeSetEvent()s a guest mixer thread, waits for it with
+// KeWaitForMultipleObjects and then submits the frame. On the console at
+// 1020 MHz the callback took 2.65-2.87 ms on average (5.3-5.7 ms per period,
+// over budget: 100-300 missing frames per 10 s, audible crackle at the tail of
+// explosions/magic), at 1428 MHz 2.26 ms with no misses - yet the Audio Worker
+// itself used ~2.7 % CPU. The time is spent waiting for the guest mixer, which
+// runs at REX_SWITCH_PRIO_GUEST (0x3B, time-sliced) and shares the 3 cores with
+// "Main XThread" (99 %, spin-waits) and another guest thread at 50-58 %.
+//
+// The mixer thread is a plain guest thread whose name/handle changes every run,
+// so it is detected here: events signalled by the Audio Worker are remembered,
+// and a guest thread that keeps waiting on one of them is the mixer. It is
+// raised to REX_SWITCH_PRIO_AUDIO_GUEST (0x2D): above the spinners at 0x3B so
+// it preempts them as soon as the Audio Worker wakes it, below the presenter
+// (0x2C) and audio/XMA (0x2B). 0x2D is inside the application range already
+// used by the 0x2B/0x2C host threads.
+//
+// Cores: every thread starts with the process default core as its preferred one,
+// and Horizon only migrates a ready thread to an idle core, not to a core busy
+// with a lower-priority spinner. Two mixers at 0x2D on the same preferred core
+// would then still run one after the other. With audio_parallel_client_callbacks
+// the first boosted mixer prefers core 1 and the second core 2 (preferred core
+// only, the mask is kept, so the kernel can still move them: no starvation risk).
+//
+// Risk: 0x2D is cooperative on Horizon (no time slicing), so a boosted thread
+// that spin-waits instead of blocking would keep its core from every 0x3B
+// thread. The mixer blocks on its event between frames, which is what makes
+// this safe; as guards, a thread is only boosted after kMinWaitsBeforeBoost
+// waits on an Audio Worker event, the main thread and host threads are never
+// boosted, at most kMaxBoostedThreads are, and the cvar turns it all off.
